          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Anmeldung: Registering Your Address in Germany</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Anmeldung: Registering Your Address in Germany</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">The address registration is the first official step after you move into a home in Germany. Without it, almost nothing else works: no benefits, no tax ID, no bank account, often no school place. Here is how to do it right the first time.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">28 October 2026</time></span><span>6 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>What the Anmeldung is</h2>\n<p>Everyone who lives in Germany must register their address with the local registration office (<em>Meldebehörde</em>), usually called the citizens' office (<em>Bürgerbüro</em>) or registration office (<em>Einwohnermeldeamt</em>) at the town hall. This is called <em>Anmeldung</em>. It applies to Germans and foreigners alike, to adults and to children. When you move again, you register the new address; when you leave Germany, you deregister (<em>Abmeldung</em>).</p>\n<h2>Why it matters so much</h2>\n<p>The registration certificate (<em>Meldebescheinigung</em>) you receive is one of the most requested documents in Germany. You need it for the Jobcenter, the immigration office, health insurance, a bank account, school and Kita registration, child benefit and a library card. After registration, the federal tax office automatically sends your personal tax ID (<em>Steuer-ID</em>) by post, which every employer needs.</p>\n<h2>The deadline: two weeks</h2>\n<p>You must register within two weeks after moving into a home. A missed deadline can lead to a fine, but more importantly it delays benefits and other applications. If appointments are scarce, book as soon as you know your moving date, and keep proof that you tried to get an appointment in time.</p>\n<h2>What you need to bring</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Passport or identity card of every person you register; for children, also birth certificates if available</li>\n<li>Your residence permit or the provisional certificate (<em>Fiktionsbescheinigung</em>), if you already have it</li>\n<li>The landlord's confirmation of moving in (<em>Wohnungsgeberbestätigung</em>)</li>\n<li>The registration form (<em>Anmeldeformular</em>), often available on the town's website; some offices fill it in with you</li>\n<li>Marriage certificate, if you register as a married couple</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The landlord's confirmation</h2>\n<p>By law, the landlord (or the person who provides the accommodation) must confirm in writing that you have moved in. The form contains the landlord's name and address, your address, the moving-in date and the names of the people who moved in. If you live with friends or relatives, the main tenant or owner signs it. Without this confirmation, the office cannot register you. Ask for it on the day you receive the keys.</p>\n<div class=\"zl-note zl-note--warn\"><strong>Important:</strong> never sign or buy a registration from someone who does not actually provide you with a home. Registering at an address where you do not live is illegal, and \"address selling\" offers are a known scam.</div>\n<h2>How to get an appointment</h2>\n<p>In larger towns such as Hof, you usually book online through the town's appointment system. In smaller municipalities around Helmbrechts, Münchberg or Naila, you can often call or come during opening hours. Ask whether all family members must come in person; often one adult can register the whole household with a power of attorney (<em>Vollmacht</em>) and the documents of the others.</p>\n<h2>At the citizens' office</h2>\n<p>The clerk checks your documents, enters your data in the register and prints the registration certificate. The appointment usually takes 10 to 20 minutes. Check spelling of names and dates of birth carefully before you leave. Ukrainian names are transliterated into Latin letters; use exactly the same spelling as in your passport, because differences later cause problems with banks and authorities.</p>\n<p>Some registration offices also issue the residence card or forward data to the immigration office. Ask what happens next in your town, so you know whether you need a separate appointment there.</p>\n<h2>After registration: who needs the certificate</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Jobcenter or social welfare office, to start or update benefits</li>\n<li>Immigration office, if it was not informed automatically</li>\n<li>Health insurance fund and bank</li>\n<li>Family benefits office (<em>Familienkasse</em>) for child benefit</li>\n<li>School, Kita and, later, your employer</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Make several copies or keep a clear photo on your phone. Some offices accept a copy, others want to see the original.</p>\n<h2>Children and newborns</h2>\n<p>Children are registered together with their parents, with their own passport or birth certificate. A baby born in Germany is registered automatically when the registry office (<em>Standesamt</em>) records the birth, so you do not need a separate registration. You will still receive a separate tax ID for the child, which you need for child benefit. If a child lives with only one parent, bring documents on custody if you have them.</p>\n<h2>Common problems and how to solve them</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No apartment yet:</strong> if you live in temporary or municipal accommodation, the operator provides the confirmation. Ask the social services or the reception staff.</li>\n<li><strong>Different spellings of your name:</strong> bring all documents and ask the clerk to use the passport spelling. Keep a note of the exact Latin spelling for every form.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing birth certificate for a child:</strong> explain the situation; many offices register the child with the passport and note the missing document.</li>\n<li><strong>No appointment within two weeks:</strong> keep screenshots of your booking attempts and show them at the appointment.</li>\n<li><strong>Lost registration certificate:</strong> ask the citizens' office for a new copy; a small fee may apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Main and secondary residence</h2>\n<p>Most people have one main residence (<em>Hauptwohnsitz</em>), the place where they mainly live. If you also stay regularly somewhere else, for example for work, you can register a secondary residence (<em>Nebenwohnsitz</em>). Some towns charge a second-home tax for it, so ask before registering. For benefits and the immigration office, your main residence is what counts.</p>\n<h2>Protecting your data</h2>\n<p>Your registration data can, in principle, be released to certain authorities and in limited cases to private persons. If you are at risk, for example after domestic violence or threats, you can apply for a block on information (<em>Auskunftssperre</em>) at the citizens' office. Bring any documents that show the danger, such as a police report. Counselling services for victims of violence can help with the application.</p>\n<h2>Moving again within Germany</h2>\n<p>When you move to a new apartment, you register at the new address within two weeks. Within Germany, you do not need to deregister at the old place; the new registration replaces it. If you move to another district, check first whether your residence permit contains a residence condition, and inform the Jobcenter before you move.</p>\n<h2>Leaving Germany</h2>\n<p>If you leave Germany permanently, deregister at the citizens' office. This ends obligations such as the broadcasting fee and helps avoid problems with authorities later. Inform the Jobcenter, the immigration office, your health insurance and your bank as well.</p>\n<h2>How we help</h2>\n<p>We help families prepare the documents, fill in the form, book appointments and, when possible, accompany them to the citizens' office. If your landlord does not know the confirmation form, we can explain it to them. <a href=\"request-assistance.html\">Ask for help</a>.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/online-services/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: online services and authorities</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://handbookgermany.de/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handbook Germany: registration and first steps in English</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.make-it-in-germany.com/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Make it in Germany: arriving and living in Germany</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>How long do I have to register?</summary><p>Within two weeks after moving into a home, at the citizens' office of your town.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can one person register the whole family?</summary><p>Often yes, with a power of attorney and the documents of the others. Ask your citizens' office.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What if the landlord refuses the confirmation?</summary><p>The landlord is legally obliged to provide it. Ask in writing, and contact the citizens' office if it is still refused.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Does registration cost money?</summary><p>No. The registration and the first certificate are free.</p></details>\n<details><summary>When does the tax ID arrive?</summary><p>Usually a few weeks after registration, automatically by post.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-residence-permit-24.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M6 16c1-2 5-2 6 0M14 10h4M14 13h3\"/></svg></span><h3>Your §24 Residence Permit: What You Need to Know</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-jobcenter-letters.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 7l9 6 9-6\"/></svg></span><h3>How to Read and Answer Jobcenter Letters</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-ukrainian-documents.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M6 16c1-2 5-2 6 0M14 10h4M14 13h3\"/></svg></span><h3>Keeping Your Ukrainian Documents Valid in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
