          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Pregnancy and Birth in Germany</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Pregnancy and Birth in Germany</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">Expecting a baby in a new country raises many questions. In Germany, care during pregnancy and birth is well organised and covered by health insurance. This guide walks you through the months before and after the birth.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">28 October 2026</time></span><span>5 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>The first steps when you are pregnant</h2>\n<p>When a pregnancy test is positive, book an appointment with a gynaecologist (<em>Frauenärztin</em> or <em>Frauenarzt</em>). Bring your health card. The doctor confirms the pregnancy, calculates the due date and issues the maternity record (<em>Mutterpass</em>), a small booklet in which every check-up and test is recorded. Carry it with you at all times during pregnancy, especially in the last weeks; at the hospital it tells the staff everything important.</p>\n<h2>Regular check-ups</h2>\n<p>During pregnancy you are entitled to regular check-ups, usually every four weeks and every two weeks towards the end. They include blood tests, blood pressure, weight, the baby's growth and, as a rule, three ultrasound examinations. All recommended check-ups are covered by statutory health insurance. Some practices offer extra tests that you have to pay yourself (<em>IGeL</em>); you can refuse them without any disadvantage.</p>\n<h2>Finding a midwife (Hebamme)</h2>\n<p>Midwives play a big role in Germany. They support you during pregnancy, can run some check-ups, offer birth preparation courses and, most importantly, visit you at home after the birth to check on mother and baby and help with breastfeeding. Their work is covered by health insurance. Because midwives are in high demand, start looking as early as the first months of pregnancy. Ask your gynaecologist, the hospital and other mothers for contacts.</p>\n<div class=\"zl-note\"><strong>Tip:</strong> contact several midwives at once by phone or email. Say your due date, your town and which language you speak. Some midwives in our region speak Russian or Ukrainian.</div>\n<h2>Pregnancy counselling and financial help</h2>\n<p>Pregnancy counselling centres (<em>Schwangerschaftsberatungsstellen</em>) run by organisations such as pro familia, Caritas or Diakonie advise free of charge and confidentially on health, rights, money and family planning. Through them you can apply for one-off financial help from the federal foundation \"Mother and Child\" for baby equipment, clothes or a pram, if your income is low. The application must be made during pregnancy, so contact a counselling centre early.</p>\n<h2>Support from the Jobcenter during pregnancy</h2>\n<p>If you receive Jobcenter benefits, tell the Jobcenter about the pregnancy and send a copy of the maternity record page with the due date. From the 13th week of pregnancy you can receive an additional monthly amount (<em>Mehrbedarf</em>). You can also apply for initial equipment for pregnancy and birth (<em>Erstausstattung bei Schwangerschaft und Geburt</em>), which covers maternity clothes and basic baby equipment. Apply before you buy anything.</p>\n<h2>Maternity protection at work</h2>\n<p>If you are employed, tell your employer about the pregnancy as soon as you can. From then on special protection applies: no dangerous work, no night shifts without consent, and strong protection against dismissal. Maternity leave (<em>Mutterschutz</em>) begins six weeks before the due date and lasts eight weeks after the birth, twelve weeks after premature or multiple births. During this time employees receive maternity pay (<em>Mutterschaftsgeld</em>) from the health insurance fund and a top-up from the employer.</p>\n<h2>Choosing where to give birth</h2>\n<p>Most babies in Germany are born in hospitals with a maternity ward (<em>Geburtsstation</em>). In our region, hospitals in Hof, Naila, Münchberg, Selb and Marktredwitz are options; which ones offer births can change, so check in time. Many hospitals offer information evenings and tours. Register at the hospital of your choice in the last months of pregnancy and bring your maternity record and health card. Some hospitals have staff or interpreters for Ukrainian and Russian; ask in advance.</p>\n<h2>Preparing for the birth</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Pack a hospital bag around the 36th week: documents, maternity record, health card, clothes, baby clothes for the way home.</li>\n<li>Prepare a list of phone numbers: hospital, midwife, a person who can look after older children.</li>\n<li>Get a car seat if you will drive home; it is compulsory for babies in cars.</li>\n<li>Prepare the documents needed for the birth registration (see below) and keep them in one folder.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>At the hospital</h2>\n<p>When contractions become regular or your waters break, go to the hospital or call an ambulance on 112 if needed. A midwife and a doctor accompany the birth. Pain relief is available. You may bring a companion. After the birth, mother and baby usually stay one to three days. The first examination of the baby (U1 and U2) is done in the hospital.</p>\n<h2>Registering the birth</h2>\n<p>Every birth in Germany must be registered with the registry office (<em>Standesamt</em>) of the town where the baby was born, within one week. Often the hospital sends the notification, but the parents must provide documents: passports, residence permits, birth certificates of the parents and, if married, the marriage certificate. Foreign documents often need certified translations. The registry office then issues the birth certificate (<em>Geburtsurkunde</em>), which you need for all further steps. Ask the registry office in advance which documents it needs from you, especially if some were lost.</p>\n<h2>After the birth: the important steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Health insurance: register the baby with your health insurance fund (family insurance) or through the Jobcenter.</li>\n<li>Residence document: apply for a residence document for the child at the immigration office.</li>\n<li>Jobcenter: report the birth and send the birth certificate.</li>\n<li>Child benefit: apply as soon as the baby's tax ID arrives by post.</li>\n<li>Parental allowance: apply in the first months to the Bavarian Centre for Family and Social Affairs (ZBFS), if you are eligible.</li>\n<li>Ukrainian documents: if you want, register the birth with the Ukrainian consulate to obtain Ukrainian documents for the child.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The first weeks at home</h2>\n<p>The midwife visits you at home in the first days and weeks. The paediatrician carries out the regular check-ups (U3 to U9) noted in the yellow booklet, and the vaccinations recommended in Germany. Choose a paediatric practice before the birth, because many have waiting lists. If you feel very sad, anxious or exhausted after the birth, talk to your midwife or doctor; this is common and help is available.</p>\n<h2>Emergency help during pregnancy</h2>\n<p>The national helpline \"Pregnant women in need\" answers around the clock, free and anonymously, in many languages; the number is on our <a href=\"emergency.html\">emergency page</a>. In an acute emergency, such as heavy bleeding or severe pain, call 112.</p>\n<h2>How we help</h2>\n<p>We help families find a gynaecologist and midwife, prepare applications to the Jobcenter and the foundation, collect baby equipment through our network, and prepare documents for the registry office. <a href=\"request-assistance.html\">Ask for help</a>.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.profamilia.de/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pro familia: pregnancy counselling (English)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/familie-und-kind/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: family and children</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/medizinische-versorgung/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: medical care</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>Is pregnancy care free?</summary><p>Yes. All recommended check-ups, the midwife, the birth and the hospital stay are covered by statutory health insurance.</p></details>\n<details><summary>When should I look for a midwife?</summary><p>As early as possible, ideally in the first months of pregnancy, because midwives are in high demand.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Where do I register the birth?</summary><p>At the registry office (Standesamt) of the town where the baby was born, within one week.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can I get help with baby equipment?</summary><p>Yes. Jobcenter initial equipment and the federal foundation \"Mother and Child\" through a counselling centre.</p></details>\n<details><summary>How long is maternity leave?</summary><p>Six weeks before the due date and eight weeks after the birth, twelve after premature or multiple births.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-school-kita-bavaria.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M2 9l10-5 10 5-10 5z\"/><path d=\"M6 11v5c3 2 9 2 12 0v-5\"/></svg></span><h3>Registering Your Child for School or Kita in Bavaria</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-child-benefit.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-3.5 3-6 6-6s6 2.5 6 6M15 20c0-2.5 1-4.5 4-4.5\"/></svg></span><h3>Child Benefit and Family Payments in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-clubs-volunteering.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"8\" r=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"9\" r=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-3.5 3-6 6-6s6 2.5 6 6M15 20c0-2.5 1-4.5 4-4.5\"/></svg></span><h3>Clubs and Volunteering: Finding Your Place</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
