          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Studying at a German University</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Studying at a German University</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">German universities charge no or only low tuition fees and are open to students from Ukraine. The path to a study place has its own rules, especially for school certificates and language. This guide explains the steps.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">28 October 2026</time></span><span>5 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>Types of higher education</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Universities</strong> (<em>Universität</em>): research-oriented, full range of subjects, including medicine, law and teaching.</li>\n<li><strong>Universities of applied sciences</strong> (<em>Hochschule</em>, formerly <em>Fachhochschule</em>): practical focus with internships, strong in engineering, business, IT and social work.</li>\n<li><strong>Art and music colleges</strong>: admission through an aptitude test.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Close to Upper Franconia there are the University of Applied Sciences in Hof, the University of Bayreuth and further universities in Bamberg, Coburg and Erlangen-Nuremberg. Bachelor's programmes usually take three to four years, master's programmes one to two.</p>\n<h2>Is my Ukrainian certificate enough?</h2>\n<p>To study in Germany you need a higher education entrance qualification (<em>Hochschulzugangsberechtigung</em>). A Ukrainian school-leaving certificate alone is often not enough for direct admission. Depending on your case, you may additionally need successfully completed study years at a Ukrainian university, or a preparatory course (<em>Studienkolleg</em>) with an assessment exam (<em>Feststellungsprüfung</em>). The rules depend on your certificates, grades and subject. The university's international office or uni-assist checks your individual case.</p>\n<h2>What uni-assist does</h2>\n<p>Many German universities use uni-assist, a central service that checks international applications. You upload your documents, pay a processing fee and uni-assist forwards a preliminary assessment (<em>Vorprüfungsdokumentation</em>) to the university. Some universities, however, accept applications directly. Always check on the website of your chosen programme which way applies.</p>\n<h2>Language requirements</h2>\n<p>For programmes taught in German, universities usually require German at level C1, proven by tests such as DSH, TestDaF or telc C1 Hochschule. Some programmes accept lower levels for the start, with language courses alongside. There are also programmes taught in English, especially at master's level; they require proof of English, such as IELTS or TOEFL. Plan the language exam early, because test dates fill up.</p>\n<div class=\"zl-note\"><strong>Tip:</strong> the integration course ends at B1. For university you need more. Ask the Jobcenter or employment agency about job-related or university-preparation language courses up to C1.</div>\n<h2>Application deadlines</h2>\n<p>Winter semester starts in October, summer semester in April. Typical deadlines for international applicants are 15 July for the winter semester and 15 January for the summer semester, but many universities set earlier dates, especially via uni-assist. Some subjects, like medicine, have national admission procedures with their own rules. Start your research at least six months before the semester.</p>\n<h2>Documents you usually need</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>School-leaving certificate and transcript, with certified translations</li>\n<li>Certificates and transcripts of any completed study years in Ukraine</li>\n<li>Language certificate (German or English)</li>\n<li>CV, passport and residence permit</li>\n<li>Sometimes a letter of motivation or portfolio</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If documents were lost in the war, contact the university or uni-assist. There are special procedures for refugees whose documents are incomplete.</p>\n<h2>Financing your studies: BAföG</h2>\n<p>BAföG is the state support for students. It is paid partly as a grant and partly as an interest-free loan of which only a limited part must be repaid. Whether you can receive it depends on your residence permit and other conditions; many students with temporary protection may be eligible. Apply to the BAföG office at the student services (<em>Studierendenwerk</em>) of your university. Important: students in programmes that can be funded by BAföG generally cannot receive Jobcenter benefits, so plan the change carefully and ask both offices before you enrol.</p>\n<h2>Scholarships and part-time work</h2>\n<p>The German Academic Exchange Service (DAAD) and many foundations offer scholarships, some especially for students from Ukraine. Universities themselves sometimes have emergency funds. Students may work part-time; many work as student assistants (<em>studentische Hilfskraft</em>) at the university or in mini-jobs. Check how earnings affect BAföG.</p>\n<h2>Fees and costs</h2>\n<p>Public universities in Bavaria charge no tuition fees for most programmes, but every student pays a semester fee (<em>Semesterbeitrag</em>) for student services and often a local transport ticket. Living costs include rent, health insurance and food. Students under 30 usually join student health insurance at a reduced rate; if you are still insured through family or the Jobcenter, clarify this when you enrol.</p>\n<h2>Student life and support</h2>\n<p>The student services (<em>Studierendenwerk</em>) run dormitories, cafeterias and counselling, including psychological and social counselling. The international office helps with organisational questions, and many universities have buddy programmes that connect new international students with local students. Ukrainian student groups exist at several Bavarian universities.</p>\n<h2>Already have a Ukrainian degree?</h2>\n<p>If you completed a bachelor's or specialist degree in Ukraine, you can often apply directly for a master's programme. The central office for foreign education (ZAB) issues a statement of comparability for foreign degrees, which helps with applications and job searches. For regulated professions such as medicine, law or teaching, a separate recognition procedure applies; see our guide on recognising qualifications.</p>\n<h2>Finding student housing</h2>\n<p>Dormitories of the Studierendenwerk are the cheapest option, but places are limited and allocated by waiting list, so apply as soon as you have applied to the university. Shared flats (<em>Wohngemeinschaft</em>, WG) are very common among students and are advertised on university notice boards and online portals. In smaller university towns such as Hof or Bayreuth, rents are lower than in big cities.</p>\n<h2>Continuing a Ukrainian degree</h2>\n<p>Some students continue their Ukrainian studies online. If you want to switch to a German university, the credits you earned can often be recognised. Bring module descriptions and transcripts; the examination office decides which courses count.</p>\n<h2>Alternatives to university</h2>\n<p>Not everyone needs a degree. Vocational training (<em>Ausbildung</em>) in Germany is highly respected and paid from the first month. Dual study programmes (<em>duales Studium</em>) combine a degree at a university of applied sciences with training in a company and a salary. Career counselling at the employment agency helps you compare options.</p>\n<h2>Your checklist</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose programmes and check the admission rules and language of instruction.</li>\n<li>Have your certificates assessed by the university or uni-assist.</li>\n<li>Start a language course aimed at C1 (or B2/C1 English).</li>\n<li>Apply before the deadline; keep copies of everything.</li>\n<li>Apply for BAföG and clarify Jobcenter and health insurance before enrolling.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>How we help</h2>\n<p>We help young people sort documents, find the right university contacts and prepare applications, and we connect them with students who have already taken this path. <a href=\"request-assistance.html\">Ask for help</a>.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.study-in-germany.de/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Study in Germany: the official portal (English)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.daad.de/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DAAD: German Academic Exchange Service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.uni-assist.de/en/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">uni-assist: applications of international students</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/bildung-und-forschung/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: education and research</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>Can I study with a Ukrainian school certificate?</summary><p>Often not directly. You may need completed study years in Ukraine or a Studienkolleg. The university or uni-assist checks your case.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Which German level do I need?</summary><p>For German-taught programmes usually C1, proven by DSH, TestDaF or a similar exam.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Are there tuition fees in Bavaria?</summary><p>Most public programmes have no tuition fees, but there is a semester fee.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Can I receive BAföG?</summary><p>Many students with temporary protection may be eligible. Ask the BAföG office at the Studierendenwerk.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do I keep Jobcenter benefits as a student?</summary><p>Generally not, if your programme can be funded by BAföG. Clarify before enrolling.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-first-job.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"7\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M8 7V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h4a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v2M3 12h18\"/></svg></span><h3>Finding Your First Job in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-recognition-qualifications.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"17\" r=\"3\"/><path d=\"M10 20l-1 2M14 20l1 2M7 8h10M7 11h6\"/></svg></span><h3>Getting Your Ukrainian Qualifications Recognised in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-integration-course.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><path d=\"M4 5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h13v16H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2z\"/><path d=\"M4 19V5\"/><path d=\"M8 7h7\"/></svg></span><h3>The Integration Course: Your Fastest Route to German</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
