          "_name": "article08",
          "_sourceTheme": "startm5",
          "_customHTML": "<section class=\"zl-page\">\n<div class=\"zl-wrap\">\n<nav class=\"zl-crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"index.html\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"guides.html\">Guides</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Keeping Your Ukrainian Documents Valid in Germany</li></ol></nav>\n<header>\n<span class=\"zl-tag\">Guides</span>\n<h1>Keeping Your Ukrainian Documents Valid in Germany</h1>\n<p class=\"zl-lead\">Living in Germany does not end your ties to Ukraine. Passports expire, children are born, people marry, and German offices ask for certificates. This guide explains how to keep Ukrainian documents valid, how to use them in Germany and what to do if they are lost.</p>\n<p class=\"zl-meta\"><span>Updated: <time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">28 October 2026</time></span><span>5 min read</span><span>ZusammenLeben</span></p>\n</header>\n<article class=\"zl-card zl-body\"><h2>Why valid documents matter</h2>\n<p>For German authorities, your Ukrainian passport is your main identity document. The immigration office needs it to extend your residence permit, the registry office needs it for marriage or a child's birth, and banks often ask for it. An expired passport does not end temporary protection, but it makes many procedures slower. Check the expiry dates of all family passports now and plan renewals early, because appointments are in high demand.</p>\n<h2>Renewing a passport abroad</h2>\n<p>Ukrainian citizens in Germany can apply for a new international passport at Ukrainian consulates and at passport service centres of the State Migration Service operating abroad. For Bavaria, the Consulate General in Munich is the nearest consulate. You usually need to book an appointment online, bring your old passport, your internal passport or ID card if available, and fees. Processing can take weeks or months, so apply well before the old passport expires.</p>\n<div class=\"zl-note\"><strong>Tip:</strong> make colour scans of every family member's passports, birth and marriage certificates and keep them in a secure cloud folder. Copies do not replace originals, but they make replacement much easier.</div>\n<h2>Men of military age</h2>\n<p>Since 2024, Ukrainian men aged 18 to 60 abroad must have updated military registration data to receive most consular services, including a new passport. The data can be updated in the Reserve+ app or at the territorial recruitment centre. Rules have changed several times, so check the current requirements on official Ukrainian websites before your appointment.</p>\n<h2>Consular registration</h2>\n<p>Ukrainians who stay abroad for a long time can register with the consulate (<em>consular registration</em>). This makes it easier for the consulate to help in emergencies and is required for some services. It is voluntary in most cases but recommended.</p>\n<h2>A child born in Germany</h2>\n<p>When a child is born in Germany, the hospital informs the registry office (<em>Standesamt</em>), which issues a German birth certificate. You need your passports and, if married, your marriage certificate with a translation. Children of Ukrainian parents are Ukrainian citizens; to get Ukrainian documents for the child, register the birth with the consulate, which requires the German birth certificate, usually with an apostille and translation. Read more in our <a href=\"guide-pregnancy-birth.html\">guide to pregnancy and birth</a>.</p>\n<h2>Marriage in Germany</h2>\n<p>To marry in Germany, the registry office asks for passports, birth certificates and often a certificate of no impediment to marriage. Ukrainian documents must usually be translated by a sworn translator and certified with an apostille. Start early: the registry office checks documents carefully and may ask for more papers. A marriage concluded in Germany can then be registered with the Ukrainian consulate.</p>\n<h2>The apostille</h2>\n<p>Germany and Ukraine both belong to the Hague Apostille Convention. An apostille is a stamp that confirms that a public document is genuine, so it can be used in the other country. Ukrainian civil status documents for German offices, such as birth or marriage certificates, often need an apostille issued in Ukraine. German documents for Ukrainian authorities get an apostille from the responsible German authority, for documents from the registry office usually the district government or a designated office. Ask the office in advance whether an apostille is required.</p>\n<h2>Sworn translations</h2>\n<p>German authorities accept foreign documents only with a translation by a translator sworn in Germany (<em>beeidigter</em> or <em>öffentlich bestellter Übersetzer</em>). Ukrainian translations made in Ukraine are often not accepted. A public database of court-approved translators lets you search by language and region. Prices vary; ask for a quote first. For simple documents, some offices accept international multilingual extracts, which do not need translation.</p>\n<h2>Diia and digital documents</h2>\n<p>The Diia app gives access to many Ukrainian state services online, such as certificates, tax number and some registrations. Digital documents in Diia are valid in Ukraine but not accepted as identity documents by German authorities. Still, the app is useful for ordering extracts from Ukrainian registers without travelling.</p>\n<h2>Ukrainian driving licence and other papers</h2>\n<p>A valid Ukrainian driving licence can be used in Germany during temporary protection; see our <a href=\"guide-driving-licence.html\">guide to driving licences</a>. Diplomas and professional certificates are needed for recognition; see our <a href=\"guide-recognition-qualifications.html\">guide to recognition</a>. Keep originals in a safe place and use certified copies for applications.</p>\n<h2>Lost or stolen documents</h2>\n<p>If your passport is lost or stolen in Germany, report it to the police and get a written confirmation. Inform the immigration office, because your residence permit is linked to the passport. Then apply for a new passport or, for urgent travel to Ukraine, a certificate for return at the consulate. If your German residence card is lost, the immigration office issues a new one.</p>\n<h2>Documents from occupied territories</h2>\n<p>Documents issued by the occupation authorities in temporarily occupied territories of Ukraine are not recognised by Ukraine or Germany. If a child was born there or a marriage registered there, Ukrainian courts and registry offices can establish the facts in a special procedure. Ask the consulate or a counselling service for current options.</p>\n<h2>Powers of attorney</h2>\n<p>If you need someone in Ukraine to act for you, for example to sell a flat, collect a pension or handle bank matters, you need a power of attorney. It can be certified at the Ukrainian consulate or by a German notary with an apostille and translation. Make it as specific as possible and keep a copy.</p>\n<h2>Spelling of names</h2>\n<p>Names are transliterated from Cyrillic into Latin letters, and different documents sometimes use different spellings, for example in an old and a new passport. German offices take the spelling from the passport. If spellings differ between your documents, the registry office may ask for proof that they belong to the same person. Keep old passports and ask the consulate for a certificate if needed. Use the same spelling in all German forms.</p>\n<h2>Certificates from Ukraine without travelling</h2>\n<p>Many extracts can be ordered remotely: through Diia, through relatives with a power of attorney, or through the consulate. This includes duplicate birth or marriage certificates, extracts from the civil status register and certificates of no criminal record, which some German employers or authorities ask for. Plan extra time for the apostille and the translation.</p>\n<h2>How we help</h2>\n<p>We help you book consular appointments, check which documents a German office needs, find sworn translators, prepare applications for apostilles and understand letters about missing papers. <a href=\"request-assistance.html\">Ask for help</a>.</p>\n<h2>Useful official sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.germany4ukraine.de/EN/online-services/seite_node.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Germany4Ukraine: online services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.justiz-dolmetscher.de/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Database of court-approved interpreters and translators</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://handbookgermany.de/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handbook Germany: documents and authorities</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"zl-disclaimer\">General information, not legal advice. Rules change; always check the official sources linked above.</p>\n</article>\n<div class=\"zl-faq zl-body\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<details><summary>Where can I renew my Ukrainian passport in Bavaria?</summary><p>At the Ukrainian Consulate General in Munich or a passport service centre abroad, usually with an online appointment.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Do I need an apostille for Ukrainian certificates?</summary><p>Often yes. German registry offices usually ask for an apostille and a sworn translation; ask the office first.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Are translations made in Ukraine accepted?</summary><p>Often not. German offices usually need a translator sworn in Germany.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Is Diia accepted as ID in Germany?</summary><p>No. Diia documents are not accepted as identity documents by German authorities.</p></details>\n<details><summary>What do I do if my passport is stolen?</summary><p>Report it to the police, inform the immigration office and apply for a new passport at the consulate.</p></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"zl-cta\"><h2>Need personal help?</h2><p>Our volunteers answer in Ukrainian, Russian, German and English. Free of charge.</p><div class=\"zl-btns\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--wa\" href=\"https://wa.me/380637004615\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Message us on WhatsApp</a><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--light\" href=\"Contact.html\">Contact form</a></div></div>\n<div class=\"zl-related\"><h2>More guides</h2><div class=\"zl-grid\">\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-anmeldung.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M6 16c1-2 5-2 6 0M14 10h4M14 13h3\"/></svg></span><h3>Anmeldung: Registering Your Address in Germany</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-residence-permit-24.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"11\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M6 16c1-2 5-2 6 0M14 10h4M14 13h3\"/></svg></span><h3>Your §24 Residence Permit: What You Need to Know</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n<a class=\"zl-tile\" href=\"guide-jobcenter-letters.html\"><span class=\"zl-ico\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 7l9 6 9-6\"/></svg></span><h3>How to Read and Answer Jobcenter Letters</h3><span class=\"zl-more\">Read the guide →</span></a>\n</div><p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><a class=\"zl-btn zl-btn--ghost\" href=\"guides.html\">All guides</a></p></div>\n</div>\n</section>",
